1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,468
Chapter six: limits.

2
00:00:01,788 --> 00:00:03,229
Learning is not recall.

3
00:00:03,549 --> 00:00:07,250
Every mechanism in this series improves how the table is written.

4
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:12,986
But a fixed state still holds only about d k exact pairs, per d v by d k state.

5
00:00:13,306 --> 00:00:14,905
A KV cache keeps all N.

6
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:16,407
Remember chapter one.

7
00:00:16,727 --> 00:00:21,607
A space with d k dimensions has room for only d k orthogonal keys.

8
00:00:21,927 --> 00:00:26,179
Every later mechanism kept the table cleaner, but none of them made it larger.

9
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:28,741
Here is a small simulation of that limit.

10
00:00:29,061 --> 00:00:30,540
The state is 8 by 8.

11
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:34,685
We store random key value pairs, then read every stored key back.

12
00:00:35,005 --> 00:00:38,714
With plain additive writes, the reads blur as soon as keys overlap.

13
00:00:39,034 --> 00:00:42,659
With the best possible write, an exact least squares solve,

14
00:00:42,659 --> 00:00:44,747
every read is exact up to 8 pairs.

15
00:00:45,067 --> 00:00:48,311
Past 8 pairs, both errors climb, and they keep climbing.

16
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:52,383
This is the limit no write rule escapes.

17
00:00:52,703 --> 00:00:56,956
A better write keeps the reads clean up to d k pairs, but past d k,

18
00:00:56,956 --> 00:00:59,051
every fixed state starts to blur.

19
00:00:59,367 --> 00:01:01,607
The reported numbers show the same gap.

20
00:01:01,927 --> 00:01:07,847
In one comparison of 1 billion parameter models on in context recall tasks,

21
00:01:07,847 --> 00:01:11,557
the best fixed state layer, Mesa, scores 41.79.

22
00:01:11,877 --> 00:01:14,689
A Transformer scores 52.27.

23
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,373
Going from plain additive writes to an exact solve gains about 4 points.

24
00:01:19,693 --> 00:01:22,360
Keeping the full cache gains 10.5 more.

25
00:01:25,067 --> 00:01:29,198
Log linear attention, from 2025, is a middle ground.

26
00:01:29,518 --> 00:01:33,939
Instead of one state, it keeps one state per power of two bucket of the past,

27
00:01:33,939 --> 00:01:35,087
like a Fenwick tree.

28
00:01:35,407 --> 00:01:39,754
The output adds up the read of each bucket's state, with a weight lambda for each.

29
00:01:40,867 --> 00:01:45,539
Recent tokens get small buckets, and far tokens are merged into big ones.

30
00:01:45,859 --> 00:01:47,940
Watch the buckets as tokens arrive.

31
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:55,073
At token 13, the buckets are tokens 0 to 7, 8 to 11, and 12, plus the token itself.

32
00:01:55,393 --> 00:01:56,575
That is 4 states.

33
00:01:57,883 --> 00:02:00,896
Each bucket is itself a linear attention state,

34
00:02:00,896 --> 00:02:03,524
the sum of v k transpose over its tokens.

35
00:02:03,844 --> 00:02:08,941
When a new token arrives, two buckets of the same size merge into one twice as big,

36
00:02:08,941 --> 00:02:10,415
like carrying in binary.

37
00:02:10,717 --> 00:02:18,779
After about a million tokens, two to the 20, a KV cache holds 1,048,576 records.

38
00:02:19,099 --> 00:02:22,255
Log linear attention holds at most 22 states.

39
00:02:22,575 --> 00:02:24,191
Linear attention holds 1.

40
00:02:25,483 --> 00:02:28,741
So the number of states grows with the logarithm of the length.

41
00:02:29,061 --> 00:02:31,980
Double the context, and you add just one more state.

42
00:02:32,283 --> 00:02:36,955
The reported gains in perplexity are small, and the gap to Transformers remains.

43
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:43,093
So in practice, models keep a few full attention layers for exact recall,

44
00:02:43,093 --> 00:02:45,344
and make the rest fixed state layers.

45
00:02:45,664 --> 00:02:48,833
That is the hybrid design, in Part 5 of the essay.

46
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:52,273
Tasks that need one exact detail from far back,

47
00:02:52,273 --> 00:02:55,263
like a number to repeat or a passage to copy,

48
00:02:55,263 --> 00:02:57,854
still need attention over real records.

49
00:02:58,174 --> 00:03:00,343
The many cheap state layers carry the rest.

50
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:03,816
Let's put the whole series together.

51
00:03:04,136 --> 00:03:07,829
Chapter one: linear attention turned the cache into one matrix,

52
00:03:07,829 --> 00:03:10,878
written with outer products and read with the query.

53
00:03:11,183 --> 00:03:14,627
Chapter two: decay let the state forget, at a fixed rate,

54
00:03:14,627 --> 00:03:18,372
at a rate chosen by the input, or at one rate per key channel.

55
00:03:18,683 --> 00:03:23,085
Chapter three: the delta rule read the old value and wrote only the error,

56
00:03:23,085 --> 00:03:25,286
so a single entry could be corrected.

57
00:03:25,583 --> 00:03:31,301
Chapter four: all of these became one rule, S t equals S t minus 1 times A t,

58
00:03:31,301 --> 00:03:34,493
plus W t, differing only in the transition.

59
00:03:35,583 --> 00:03:38,767
Chapter five: each write became one gradient step,

60
00:03:38,767 --> 00:03:43,669
which opened the door to richer models, better optimizers, and better losses.

61
00:03:44,983 --> 00:03:45,781
To recap.

62
00:03:46,101 --> 00:03:49,022
A fixed state can be written better and better,

63
00:03:49,022 --> 00:03:53,187
but it holds only about d k exact pairs, while a cache holds all N.

64
00:03:54,083 --> 00:03:57,283
Left over: exact recall still needs real records.

65
00:03:57,603 --> 00:04:02,229
That is why hybrids mix a few full attention layers with many fixed state layers.

66
00:04:02,549 --> 00:04:03,705
Thanks for watching.
